                        "review": "---READ THIS BEFORE YOU BUY---\n\nAlright, here's the deal. This game is phenomenal, and I want you to buy it. However, I've seen far too many people have a bad time initially because they have the wrong expectations going into it. If you ask around in this game's community, you'll find that It's actually an almost universal experience to try this game for a few hours, get frustrated, and leave, only to come back to it weeks or months later as if compelled by some mysterious force, and only then begin to fall in love with the game. That's what happened to me, after all.\n\nI'm hoping that, by straddling the line between setting your expectations and avoiding spoilers, I might be able to save you some of that frustration.\n\nQ1. Don't worry, I'm already a fan of difficult 2D platformers. I've played Hollow Knight, how hard can this be?\nA. Yeah, Rain World is a difficult game when it wants to be, but that's not the issue. So, you know how, with Dark Souls, a lot of people get frustrated when trying the game for the first time because they've played Skyrim, or Diablo, and expect the game to be like that? Rain world has a similar problem. A lot of people come into this game after playing Hollow Knight, or other atmospheric 2D platformers with high difficulty, and that shapes their expectations in the wrong way. All of those games manage the difficulty of each section with pinpoint precision to create the perfect challenge. Enemies walk on predictable patrol paths, or simply stay in one place, to ensure that each fight is a challenge that is difficult but fair. Rain World doesn't do any of that.\n\nInstead, the game simulates an ecosystem mude up of many different creatures, and each of those creatures navigate the world in very much the same way that you do. They go from room to room, exploring the area while looking for food to eat, lazing around if they're full, chasing any prey that will fit in their mouth, and running from larger predators. Some creatures also have other things that they want to accomplish during the day that are unique to them. Needless to say, this idea of providing a challenge that is \"difficult but fair\" is substantially complicated by the creatures in this game having such an unnerving amount of free will.\n\nSometimes, you will ento a room only to be snatched up instantly by a predator that just happened to be standing by the entrance. Other times, you will make it all the way from one side of an area to the other and be amazed at how you haven't seen a single predator. And occasionally, you will be sure that you're about the die, only for a predator to give you a free pass for reasons that only it understands. So yeah, the issue with this game is not the difficulty, It's that this game is not fair, and it's not really supposed to be, either. You sort of have to go into this expecting the occasional utter bullshit.\n\nQ2. I'm confused, Where am I supposed to go? What is my objective?\nA. Initially, you're objective is just to survive. Wake up. Find food. Don't become food. Make it back to the shelter before nightfall. Simple stuff. Of course, it always helps to explore the area in order to find new sources of food, because that will make it easier to survive.\n\nHowever, if you ever find yourself wanting more, than i suppose you could always try exploring a little further. It couldn't hurt, right? I mean, you've already mastered surviving in this little nook of the map, don't you want to find out what's over there? What about over here? What does this thing do? How do I use it? Can I eat it? What is that thing? Is it dangerous? Why is this here? I wonder what this place used to be? What happens if I go this way? Where does it lead? What's the worst that could happen?\n\n...Erm, so yeah. There's your gameplay loop. So enjoy dying like 20 times trying to get somewhere only for it to be a dead end and still feel that somehow it was worth it anyway. That happens sometimes. At least you've filled out that part of the map now.\n\nQ3. Why is the movement system so ass?\nA. Yeah, so one of the things this game does is it has everything use procedural animations, including you. It works well for what the developers were going for, but it also means that traversing the world is equally difficult for you and everthing else. You start to get the hang of it after about 20 hours or so, so you'll just have to put up with it until then.\n\nThe game is also criminally deceptive with how it teaches you the controls, because there is an entire wiki page of like forty different movement techs you can do that the game never teaches you. Thankfully, the vast majority of them are not required for beating the game, so don't worry about them. I only ever ended up learning a handful that were both useful and easy to pull off. I would still look them up after a while just to see what kind of things are possible, because some of it is nuts. \n\nI will tell you one important thing about the controls, though. You can control whether you are standing up or laying down by using up and down inputs on your keyboard or controller of choice. That's actually really important, because you move differently depending on whether you are standing up or laying down, and you have to use both to meaningfully traverse the world. There are also some things that will force you to stand up or lay down, so a huge part of mastering movement in this game is switching between these two different movement systems. Just telling you because the game amazingly never teaches you this, so I've seen a couple people get frustrated about how their slugcat is taking too long to stand up on their own, since they haven't realized that they can control that yet.\n\nQ4. This place is lonely, are there any NPCs I can talk to in this game?\nA. You'll have to find that out on your own.\n\nQ5. Does this game have an ending, or any concrete objectives?\nA. You'll have to find that out on your own.\n\nTL;DR: 0/10, Rain World truly is the worst amusement park.",
